{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788389484,"nonce":"s3X3kfUx1ej7kyqGOFYpcux07Gs=","title":"IR dialect has no per-element stuff channel, so one place-layout call cannot give a layout two wall materials","message":"Found designing RUNS/openrun-20260902/andbourne-ii.ir.json (Andbourne II).\n\n## The gap\n\nThe design wants GRANITE on the 144-cell perimeter (MaxHitPoints x1.7, where\nthings get shot) and SANDSTONE on the 211 interior wall cells (WorkToBuild x5.0\nvs granite's x6.0, and Beauty x1.1). Both are the same def: `Wall`.\n\n`--stuff-map` is keyed by **def name** (`rwa/rwa` cmd_place_layout, and\ntemplates/INDEX.md \"Parameters\"): `{\"Wall\": \"BlocksGranite\"}` binds every Wall\nelement in the payload. There is no way to say \"these cells granite, those\nsandstone\" in one call.\n\nNor can `--layer` do it: layer order is PINNED (templates/INDEX.md pin 3,\nlayers[0] = buildings and furniture, layers[1] = conduits), so a third\n\"material\" layer would break the dialect.\n\n## Why it is nearly free to fix\n\n**The mod side already supports it.** `rwa/rwa`'s own docstring for the stuff\nresolution says: \"an element's own `stuff` beats both [the map and `*`]\", and\neach placement publishes `stuff_source` with `element` as one of its values. So\n`place-layout` on the Verse side already reads a per-element `stuff` field.\n\nWhat is missing is only the two steps in front of it:\n\n  * the IR has no channel to carry it, and\n  * `ir_elements()` in `rwa/rwa` never emits `stuff` on an element.\n\n## Proposed shape\n\nA `stuff` grid beside `terrain`, same row-major shape, `.` meaning \"not\nspecified, fall through to --stuff-map / * / game default\":\n\n    \"stuff\": [[\"BlocksGranite\", \".\", ...], ...rows]\n\n`ir_elements()` reads `ir[\"stuff\"][r][c]` for each layer-0 token and sets\n`el[\"stuff\"]` when it is not `.`. Precedence is then exactly what the client\nalready documents: element \u003e --stuff KEY=DEF \u003e --stuff-map \u003e `*` \u003e game default.\n`ir.py`'s to_xml/parse_xml gain a `stuffGrid` element to keep the round trip\nhonest, or the field is declared IR-only and documented as a lossy edge\nalongside the three `ir.py` already names.\n\n## Workaround until then\n\nPlace with `--stuff Wall=BlocksGranite` and accept 1,055 blocks of interior\nwall in granite instead of sandstone (+20% wall work, +21% HP), or split the\nlayout into a shell file and an interior file and place it in two calls.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n1. `baseviz/ir.py` documents a `stuff` grid: same `size` as `layers[0]`, `.` =\n   unspecified, tokens are stuff defNames. `load_ir` on a file without the key\n   still works (absent == all `.`).\n2. `rwa place-layout \u003cf\u003e --origin P --print-payload` emits `\"stuff\": \"\u003cdef\u003e\"`\n   on exactly those elements whose grid cell is not `.`, and omits the key\n   otherwise.\n3. A per-element `stuff` beats `--stuff KEY=DEF`, which beats `--stuff-map`,\n   which beats `*`. Proven by a print-payload check, not by reading the code.\n4. `--strict-stuff` still refuses only elements that end with no stuff from any\n   source; an element carrying its own `stuff` is never refused by it.\n5. A two-material layout places in ONE call: a fixture with granite perimeter\n   and sandstone interior walls goes through `--dry-run` clean and its published\n   `stuff_source` reads `element` for the named cells and `stuff_map`/`stuff_map:*`\n   for the rest.\n6. `templates/INDEX.md` \"Parameters\" is updated: stuff is bound at placement OR\n   pinned per cell in the IR, with the precedence order written out. The\n   sentence \"tokens are bare defNames ... material is bound at placement via\n   --stuff-map\" stays true and gains the exception.\n7. `RUNS/openrun-20260902/andbourne-ii.ir.json` gains its stuff grid and its\n   .md section 13 item 2 retires.","files":null}]}